This is the SQL equivalent of Pandas .groupby(). 
Split rows into groups, apply an aggregate to each group.



-- Total revenue by product

SELECT product,
       SUM(amount) AS total_revenue
FROM orders
GROUP BY product;



-- Order count and revenue by status

SELECT status,
       COUNT(*) AS order_count,
       SUM(amount) AS total_revenue
FROM orders
GROUP BY status;



-- Average order value by customer

SELECT customer_id,
       COUNT(*) AS order_count,
       ROUND(AVG(amount),2) AS average_order_value,
       SUM(amount) AS total_spent
FROM orders
GROUP BY customer_id
ORDER BY total_spent DESC;



-- Group by multiple columns

SELECT status,
       product,
       COUNT(*) AS count
FROM orders
GROUP BY status, product
ORDER BY product, count DESC;



The rule: 
Any column in SELECT that is not inside an aggregate function
must appear in GROUP BY. 
This is the most common SQL error beginners make.



-- WRONG — name is not aggregated and not in GROUP BY

SELECT name, SUM(amount)
FROM orders
GROUP BY product;



-- RIGHT

